00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.790 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone thanks for coming  as alex mentioned my name is lara acken  and i'm an assistant professor  of social psychology at simon fraser  university  but i'm also a wife a mom to an  extremely handsome english bulldog  and a big sister to this big fella my  baby brother josh  now years ago when josh and i were  little we used to spend a lot of time  playing games  together and while i like to think that  i was a kind big sister  i have a striking memory of a nasty game  i used to play on my brother  back when he was less of a physically  threatening force  and this game wasn't so much a game as  it was a trick which involved me telling  josh  that nickels were worth more than dimes  because coins were valued based on their  size  and now to make my case i pointed  towards consistent and familiar evidence  for instance i reminded josh that  loonies are bigger than quarters  and also worth more josh agreed  similarly i reminded him that quarters  are bigger than nickels and also worth  more  josh agreed so then i went on to tell  him that it followed obviously that  because nickels were bigger than dimes  they were also worth more luckily for me  josh agreed  so i traded him handfuls of nickels for  dimes  essentially doubling my worth which was  probably four dollars at the time  and marched straight down the street to  the corner store to buy bags of penny  candy  because i thought that would make me  happy now  why do i tell you the story well it's  not to illustrate that i'm a bad person  or a terrible big sister but to  illustrate there are times when we as  human beings act in selfish ways  to further our own self-interest here i  lied to my baby brother to earn extra  cash  because i wanted to buy myself something  that i thought would make me happy  i acted in accordance with what some  might call my selfish genes and try to  improve my stake  at the cost at some of someone else but  does this childish tale of trickery  deceit and sugar sum up our human  existence  well i would argue that no it doesn't so  every day we can look around the world  around us and see kind acts both large  and small  so for instance an extreme example might  be the actions of ken  taylor the canadian ambassador to iran  who sheltered six us diplomats  during the hostile takeover and  revolution in 1980  and while taylor's actions are extremely  heroic we can see smaller scale acts  every day  from the surprise bouquet of flowers we  get from our friends and our or our  spouse  to the free cup of coffee left for us at  a local tim hortons which i did not  receive today but i  haven't purchased a coffee so maybe  that's why  um anyway point is the point is is that  small acts of giving in kindness are  indeed all around us  and in my brief time with you today i'd  like to argue that these acts of  kindness are not just good for the  recipient as we might suspect  but that helping others can actually  increase our own well-being  so i started off studying this question  during my graduate studies while working  with elizabeth dunn and mike norton  trying to investigate how people can  kind of gain the most happiness from  their everyday spending choices  and to starting off investigating this  question  liz mike and myself conducted a large  nationally representative survey  of over 600 americans and we asked them  to tell us  two separate pieces of information first  we asked for information about how  much they spend in an average month on  four spending categories  so the first was their expenses rent  bills and debt  the second was gifts for themselves the  third was gifts for others  and the fourth was donations to charity  we also asked people to tell us how  happy they were  on a one item happiness scale now to get  an idea of how much money people spent  on themselves and others in an average  month  we sum together these four spending  categories into two spending indices  so what i mean by that is we created an  index of what i'll call  personal spending by summing together  how much money people spent on  expenses rent bills and debt along with  gifts for themselves  telling how much went to themselves and  similarly we created an index of what  i'll call  pro-social spending which is how much  money people put in an  average month towards others so for  donations to charity or gifts to other  people  then we simply asked how do these two  spending indices relate to people's  well-being  well we did some pretty simple  statistics and came up with some pretty  striking results  what we found was first and foremost how  much money people spent on themselves in  an  average month was pretty much unrelated  to their own well-being  so pouring tons of money into gifts for  yourself for your own everyday expenses  didn't seem to really pay off in terms  of happiness  interestingly however how much money  people spend on  others did indeed predict their  happiness such that spending more on  others was associated with higher levels  of happiness  and i should note that this was true  across the income spectrum so this  suggests that how much money we spend on  others  might be associated with higher levels  of happiness  but to find out whether giving or  spending on others actually causes  higher levels of happiness  we followed up with an experiment in  which i went out in the morning hours on  campus  recruited a bunch of students and gave  them an envelope with either a five or  twenty dollar bill  that they were told they had to spend  that day now half of the students were  told to put this money towards something  for themselves  so they were told to cover a bill  expense or go out and buy themselves a  gift  the other half were told that they had  to use this money for someone else  so they had to put it towards a gift for  someone else or a donation to charity  now importantly everybody was told to  spend the money by five o'clock  so that a research assistant could call  them in the evening and find out how  they were feeling  so what did we find well we found that  people who were randomly assigned to  spend money on others were significantly  happier at the end of the day  here it was regardless of whether they  were spending five or twenty dollars  those who spent on others were happier  now an important question i think we can  raise from this is whether these  findings i've reported so far  something that might be unique to north  america where  a number of people might report having  extra disposable income  that they can choose to spend on others  but what happens if we go to other  countries and cultures around the world  where people might have less money to  spend and might presumably get the most  benefit from spending on themselves  well to address this question i  partnered with an international  collaborative team and a fantastic  economist who you'll hear from later  john and we went to countries to we went  to we collected a sample  of data from canada a relatively rich  nation  but also from a relatively uh poor  sample in south africa  and all these participants came into the  lab they were asked to fill out some  questionnaires and they were told that  in exchange for the time  they had earned an additional study  payment that they could use to buy  a goodie bag filled with treats like  chocolate or juice  now half of the students were told that  these goodie bags would be for them and  available for pickup at the conclusion  of the experiment  but the other half were told that their  goodie bag would be donated to a  sick kid at a local children's hospital  now  everybody had the option to opt in or  opt out of purchasing this goodie bag  but almost everybody decided to purchase  it  and then we asked them how are you  feeling afterward well what we found was  the same as what we had seen in north  america before  people who were randomly assigned to use  their money to purchase a goodie bag for  someone else  were happier than those who were  assigned to buy the same goodie bag for  themselves  now this is true even though  participants who were giving the goodie  bag away essentially  had no contact with their recipient and  importantly this was true  both in canada where a large proportion  of our students reported that they had  extra disposable income  but also in south africa where a  sizeable proportion of our sample  reported difficulty securing food for  themselves and their family in the past  12 months  so looking beyond canada and south  africa we were able to analyze data from  over 230 000 participants in 136  countries  by looking at data from the gallup world  poll and what we did was ask a simple  and similar question which  is whether donating money to charity in  the last  12 in the last month is associated with  higher levels of happiness  what we found was similar to what we had  seen before  such that in 88 of the country surveyed  we found a positive relationship between  donating money to charity and happiness  now these findings can actually be seen  in this world map here  where countries shown in green indicate  a positive relationship  between donating money to charity and  happiness while countries shown in red  indicate a negative relationship  the darker the color the stronger the  relationship and as you can see  the world is mainly green suggesting a  pervasive link between giving and  happiness  around the world all right so by now  i've hopefully started to convince you  that giving to others has  measurable and observable benefits for  our own well-being  but at what age do we as human beings  start to find giving rewarding  well there's some new research emerging  that indicating that young kids like to  engage  or sorry begin engaging in sharing  behavior and helpful behavior  spontaneously and they start to do so  quite early in life as early as 18  months young kids are offering to help  other people  and in fact sometimes they'll do it when  it involves some active self-sacrifice  such as  getting up and walking away from a fun  game to go help someone in need  now why are young kids engaging in this  potentially costly helping behavior  well i'd like to argue that just like  the adults i've been talking about so  far  young kids might also feel good about  giving  so to test this question i partnered  with a developmental psychologist  kylie hamlin and elizabeth dunn another  social psychologist to explore this  question  and what we did was we recruited a  sample of 20 toddlers just under the age  of two  and we asked them to come into the lab  with a parent or a caregiver  and they receded across the table from  an experimenter who  introduced them to a puppet named monkey  who was a monkey  and they were told that monkey liked  eating treats  then each child was given their own  little plastic bowl  and eight edible treats stuff that kids  really like like goldfish crackers or  teddy graham crackers  then over the next few minutes each kid  was taken through a series of giving  ex giving stages if you will first they  were asked to watch is the experimenter  gave a treat to monkey  then they were asked to give one of the  experimenters treats to monkey  and finally they were asked to give one  of their own treats to monkey  now every time monkey received a treat  he ate it in a large and very showy way  so he leaned forward shoved his face in  the bowl and went  and appeared to eat the treat now  obviously you as adults know that a  stuffed animal monkey can't consume a  treat  but we convinced the kids that he could  by pushing the treat through a false  bottom corner in the bowl making it  disappear  making it look as if monkey had actually  eaten the tree  and we wanted to know how the kids  responded to these different giving and  receiving opportunities  so we captured their expressions on  videotape and later coded their faces  for how much  how much happiness was present or to  what extent they were smiling  we of course were interested in whether  kids were happier giving treats away  that they were when receiving treats  and whether the emotional benefits of  giving might differ on account of  whether they were giving their own food  or food that kind of belonged to the  experimenter so what did we find  well first and foremost we found that  kids did smile more  when giving treats away to others than  they did when receiving treats  themselves  and this was true both when they gave a  treat that belonged to the experimenter  or when they gave an identical treat  from their own stash  but what's even more exciting is that  kids smiled  much larger or showed more happiness if  you will when they gave away their own  treat than when they gave away the same  identical treat  that belonged to the experimenter so  this might suggest that giving is  actually especially rewarding  when it involves some act of  self-sacrifice  all right so um the simple take-home  message i'd like to leave you with today  is that giving to others can actually  promote our own well-being  but before i say goodbye or before i  conclude i'd just like to address the  very real possibility that some of you  are sitting here thinking  well that's all well and good but i kind  of had an intuition about this to begin  with  i may have not had slides or a  powerpoint available to make my case  but i've been thinking about this too  and well to that i say kudos  good for you but it turns out we've  asked a large sample of over a hundred  adults  to tell us their predictions on the  matter and when we do so we find that  the significant majority seem to think  that they'll get larger emotional  rewards  from spending on themselves than from  than on others  now in a weird way this makes me feel a  little bit better because it suggests my  eight-year-old self wasn't some  deviant who thought that stealing money  from her brother  to buy candy for herself would make her  happier i wasn't alone is the point i  was saying  most adults seem to hold a similar  intuition that spending money on  themselves will make them happier  however what i think is better news is  that  armed with a little bit of knowledge we  can correct these intuitions  so little me is all grown up and i've  learned my lesson  and now i know that if what if happiness  is really what i'm after  rather than steal my brother's money to  buy myself candy  it would be oh so much sweeter to give  him some so that's exactly what i've  done  thank you  you

